  <section class="sec" style="padding-top:36px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <article class="article">
        <p class="lede">Most cost comparisons get this wrong because they compare a remote professional's monthly rate against an in-house employee's salary. Salary is not the cost of an employee. It is roughly 60 to 70 percent of it. Here is the full line-by-line comparison, using a mid-level administrative and operations role as the example.</p>

        <h2>What an in-house hire really costs</h2>
        <p>Take a US administrative or operations professional at a $55,000 salary. Before that person does any work, the employer adds:</p>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Typical annual cost</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Base salary</td><td>$55,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Payroll taxes (Social Security, Medicare, unemployment)</td><td>$4,500 to $5,500</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Health insurance and benefits</td><td>$8,000 to $13,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Retirement contributions</td><td>$1,500 to $3,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Recruiting (ads, tools, or agency fee)</td><td>$2,000 to $8,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Equipment, software seats, workspace</td><td>$2,500 to $5,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Onboarding and management time</td><td>$2,000 to $4,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Fully loaded total</strong></td><td><strong>$75,500 to $93,500</strong></td></tr>
        </table>
        <p>US Bureau of Labor Statistics data consistently shows benefits alone adding roughly 38 to 40 percent on top of wages for private industry employers. That is why finance teams use a loaded cost multiplier of 1.3 to 1.5 times salary. The honest comparison number for this role is about $6,300 to $7,800 a month, not the $4,583 the salary alone suggests.</p>

        <h2>What the same role costs as a dedicated remote professional</h2>
        <p>A dedicated remote professional at the Professional tier is $2,500 a month, or $30,000 a year, as a single invoice. That figure already includes:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Recruiting, screening, and skills testing</li>
          <li>Employment contract, payroll, and local compliance</li>
          <li>Benefits administration in the professional's home country</li>
          <li>A replacement guarantee if the hire does not work out</li>
          <li>Ongoing HR support from the staffing partner</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Your remaining costs are the tools you choose to give them access to and your own management time, which is lighter because the role starts with documented processes and a structured first month. Even adding a generous allowance for both, the total lands near $32,000 to $35,000 a year for the same full-time capacity.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <p><strong>The headline math:</strong> for this example role, in-house costs roughly $75,500 to $93,500 a year fully loaded. The dedicated remote equivalent costs roughly $30,000 to $35,000 a year all in. That is a saving of about 55 to 65 percent, before counting the value of hiring in weeks instead of months.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Side by side: the full picture</h2>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Factor</th><th>In-house hire</th><th>Dedicated remote professional</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Annual cost (example role)</td><td>$75,500 to $93,500 loaded</td><td>$30,000 to $35,000 all in</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Time to hire</td><td>6 to 12 weeks typical</td><td>About 5 weeks, first call to onboarded</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Recruiting fees</td><td>Ads, tools, or 15 to 25% agency fee</td><td>Included</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Payroll and compliance</td><td>Your responsibility</td><td>Handled by the partner</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Replacement if it fails</td><td>Restart the search, at your cost</td><td>Guaranteed replacement included</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Scaling up or down</td><td>Slow, with severance and legal exposure</td><td>Adjust month to month</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Office and equipment</td><td>Your cost</td><td>No office cost</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Control over the work</td><td>Full</td><td>Full: they work your hours, in your tools, for you only</td></tr>
        </table>

        <h2>Where in-house still wins</h2>
        <p>This comparison is not an argument that every role should be remote. In-house is the right call when the role needs physical presence, hands-on equipment, in-person client work, or security clearances tied to a location. Leadership roles that carry the company's culture and strategy also usually belong in the room. The strongest setups are often hybrid in structure: a local core team, extended by dedicated remote professionals in operations, support, finance, and marketing execution. That is the model our <a href="../../services/staffing-recruitment/">staffing and recruitment</a> team builds most often.</p>

        <h2>The costs people forget on the remote side too</h2>
        <p>Honesty cuts both ways. A remote professional is not zero-effort:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Management time is real.</strong> Expect a weekly check-in and clear written priorities. Budget an hour a week, not zero.</li>
          <li><strong>Documentation matters.</strong> Roles with written processes ramp in days. Roles that live in someone's head ramp slowly in any country, including yours.</li>
          <li><strong>Time zones need a decision.</strong> Our professionals work your business hours, but your team still needs agreed overlap for meetings, which we cover in the <a href="../../blog/nearshore-vs-offshore-vs-onshore/">nearshore vs offshore vs onshore guide</a>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Even fully counted, these costs are small next to the loaded-cost gap. The detailed role-by-role numbers are in our <a href="../../blog/virtual-assistant-cost/">virtual assistant cost guide</a>.</p>

        <h2>How to run this comparison for your own role</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Take the role's salary and multiply by 1.4 for a realistic loaded cost.</li>
          <li>Add one-time recruiting and onboarding costs, spread over the expected tenure.</li>
          <li>Get the dedicated remote quote for the same seniority: ours are $1,500, $2,500, and from $5,000 a month by tier, listed on the <a href="../../#pricing">pricing section</a>.</li>
          <li>Compare annual totals, then weigh the non-cost factors: speed, flexibility, and replacement risk.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>For most back-office and execution roles, the math is not close. The remaining question is whether the role is ready to be delegated, which is exactly what a discovery call is for.</p>
      </article>
